        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Calculations have shown that during extravehicular activity (EVA) astronauts receive additional radiation exposure to the skin, eyes, blood forming organs, and other radiosensitive organs compared to the exposure they would have received had they remained inside the Shuttle crew compartment. This additional exposure is due to the reduced radiation shielding provided by the Extravehicular Mobility Unit (EMU) compared with the crew compartment. The dose enhancement is greatest for the skin due to the ambient flux of low energy particles outside the spacecraft. The majority of low energy particles are encountered during traversal through the South Atlantic Anomaly (SAA).  The SAA is a kidney-bean shaped region over the South Atlantic Ocean between Brazil and South Africa where the inner trapped proton belt dips to low altitudes. Increased radiation exposure results in an increased risk of cancer and cataract development.
<p>
Legal and ethical requirements require that the radiation exposure received by astronauts be measured as accurately as possible. The current Crew Passive Dosimeter (CPD) location in in the EMU is a pocket on the right side of the upper-torso portion of the Liquid Cooling Garment (LCG). The astronaut's right upper arm shields this location. The additional shielding provided by the arm may result in artificially low exposure measurements. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
During EVA preparations, seven dosimeter assemblies were placed on the crewmember at specific locations prior to donning the comfort lining.  The specific locations were: 1) Center of forehead (between communication cap and sweat band), 2) Left mid-deltoid, 3) Right mid-deltoid, 4) Mid-sternum (midway between sternal notch and xyphoid process), 5) Left hip (midway between greater trochanter of femur and wing of ileum), 6) Right hip (midway between greater trochanter of femur and wing of ileum), and 7) Mid-back (centerline at chest level).  One dosimeter remained inside the Orbiter to serve as a background control. The Crew Passive Dosimeter (CPD) was stowed in the nominal location in the Liquid Cooling Garment (LCG). After completion of the EVA, the dosimeter assemblies were removed from the crewmember, replaced in a zip-lock bag with the control dosimeter, and returned to their stowage locker. New dosimeter assemblies were used for each EVA.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The preliminary results from the STS-61 EVA dosimetry measurements show that the EVA crew received no measurable additional exposure during the EVAs. The projected small additional exposure is too small to resolve from the approximately 1500 mrad background exposure. The lack of additional crew exposure is the result of the EVAs occurring mostly during non-SAA pass orbits. Two EVA orbits did encounter the SAA: one orbit during EVA 1 grazed the edge of the SAA; the crew reentered the airlock at the end of EVA 5 shortly after the Orbiter began an SAA pass.
<p>
All of the EVA crew CPD measurements were lower than the non-EVA crew readings. This resulted from storing the CPDs inside the heavily shielded airlock during the crew’s sleep periods. Passage through the SAA, the major source of radiation exposure for this mission, occurred during the crew’s sleep periods.
<p>
        </div>

